= Dynamic random-access memory — Technical =
DRAM is a volatile charge-storage technology. A conventional one-transistor/one-capacitor cell is accessed through a word line and bit line; shared sense amplifiers detect and restore the small cell signal. The physical device, controller and interface together determine observable behavior. DDR5 examples below must not be projected onto LPDDR, graphics DRAM, HBM or older DDR generations without checking their own specifications.<ref name="jedectech">JEDEC, [https://www.jedec.org/standards-documents JESD79-5 DDR5 SDRAM standards family]; revision-specific timing and commands are normative.</ref><ref name="microntech">Micron Technology, [https://www.micron.com/products/memory/dram-components DRAM data sheets and product resources], part-specific limits.</ref>
== Charge and sensing ==
A simplified isolated capacitor obeys <math>Q=CV</math>. Under a hypothetical constant leakage current, <math>\Delta Q\approx I_{\rm leak}\Delta t</math>, giving <math>\Delta V\approx I_{\rm leak}\Delta t/C</math>. This is a dimensional illustration, not a retention specification: real leakage depends on temperature, voltage, manufacturing variation and history. Bit-line capacitance, sensing noise and nonlinear leakage invalidate the isolated-cell picture for predicting a real refresh limit.
=== Independently reproducible hypothetical example ===
Assume <math>C=20\,\mathrm{fF}</math>, <math>I_{\rm leak}=1\,\mathrm{fA}</math> and <math>\Delta t=1\,\mathrm{ms}</math>. Then <math>\Delta Q=10^{-18}\,\mathrm C</math> and <math>\Delta V=5\times10^{-5}\,\mathrm V=50\,\mathrm{\mu V}</math>. These invented values are not measurements, design targets or safe refresh intervals.
== Commands, banks and row buffers ==
ACTIVATE connects a row to the bank's sense amplifiers. READ/WRITE transfer selected columns; PRECHARGE prepares the bank for another row. Timing constraints include activation-to-column delay (<math>t_{RCD}</math>), precharge (<math>t_{RP}</math>), active-row minimum (<math>t_{RAS}</math>) and row cycle (<math>t_{RC}</math>). Device generations and speed bins differ. Bank groups, burst transfers, command turnaround and power-state transitions impose further constraints.
For a simplified row-conflict sequence, the earliest column-data dependency may include <math>T\gtrsim t_{RP}+t_{RCD}+t_{CL}</math>, where <math>t_{CL}</math> represents a column-access latency. This is not a complete CPU load-to-use latency equation. An invented 15 ns for each term yields 45 ns, versus an illustrative 15 ns row-hit column latency; the 30 ns difference is arithmetic, not an empirical hardware comparison. Controller queues, clock domains and overlapping operations can change observed outcomes.
== Refresh and retention distributions ==
Refresh restores cell information before retention failures within the specified conditions. Different refresh modes and operating-temperature regimes have different requirements. Retention is variable across cells and operating conditions, so a typical cell cannot set the worst-case safe interval. A controller must account for refresh interference with ordinary traffic, and a software process cannot assume that its physical rows stay active.
== Bandwidth and latency ==
For a data interface of width <math>w</math> bits transferring <math>f</math> times per second, an idealized data-only peak rate is <math>R=fw/8</math> bytes/s. At hypothetical <math>f=3.2\times10^9\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}</math> and <math>w=64</math>, <math>R=25.6\times10^9</math> bytes/s = 25.6 GB/s (decimal). Real sustained throughput depends on commands, refresh, bank conflicts, address mapping, controller policy and workload. A peak interface figure is not application bandwidth.
Row-buffer locality, bank-level parallelism and read/write turnaround can dominate access patterns. Open-page and close-page scheduling have workload-specific tradeoffs; neither is universally optimal.
== Reliability and security ==
Errors can arise from retention, signal integrity, defective cells, radiation or disturbance. Kim and colleagues experimentally demonstrated that repeated activations could induce flips in other rows of susceptible DRAM devices.<ref name="hammertech">Yoongu Kim et al., [https://doi.org/10.1145/2678373.2665726 “Flipping Bits in Memory Without Accessing Them: An Experimental Study of DRAM Disturbance Errors”], ISCA (2014).</ref> Mitigations include device/controller controls, refresh policies and error correction, but efficacy must be evaluated on the actual platform. ECC does not guarantee correction of arbitrary multibit errors.
== Engineering distinctions preserved from the canonical overview ==
A row activation connects cells to shared sense amplifiers, whose detection is coupled to charge restoration. READ and WRITE select columns, while PRECHARGE prepares the bank for a subsequent activation. A row-buffer hit can avoid a new activation; conflicting requests, refresh, bank scheduling and bus turnaround complicate latency. Ordinary DRAM loses reliable contents without power. SRAM retains a powered bistable state without conventional DRAM-style capacitor refresh; flash uses nonvolatile program/erase mechanisms. Row-disturbance results are device-dependent, not a universal property of every chip.

== Historical and methodological boundaries ==
Historical priority claims distinguish concept, patent, working prototype and commercial product. DRAM generations have different interfaces and timing contracts; JEDEC and part-specific documentation must be cited by edition for compliance assertions. Experiments should record part number, controller, temperature, power state, refresh mode, workload, queue depth and measurement uncertainty. Cache and prefetch effects must be excluded when interpreting a DRAM benchmark.
The proposed [[Computer memory]] article (PR #96) treats the broader memory taxonomy; [[Computer architecture]] explains system placement. This account treats the DRAM-specific implementation, not a universal law of computer memory.
